Implement the Verilog module based on the following description.
Consider this Verilog module "dff_module":

// module dff_module (
//     input clk,
//     input reset,
//     input d,
//     output reg q
// );

// always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
//     if (reset) begin
//         q <= 0;
//     end else begin
//         q <= d;
//     end
// end

// endmodule

// Create a hierarchical Verilog design where the D flip-flop and reset block are in a submodule, and that submodule is instantiated in this code. Create the submodule called "top_dff_module".

module top_dff_module(
    input clk,
    input reset,
    input d,
    output reg q);

Implement a frequency divider that takes an input clock signal and produces an output clock signal with a frequency that is reduced by a factor specified by the parameter 'div'. When the reset signal is asserted, the output clock and internal counter are initialized to zero. On each positive edge of the input clock, the module increments the counter until it reaches the value of 'div - 1', at which point it toggles the output clock signal and resets the counter. This design effectively halves the input clock frequency when div is set to 2, or allows other divisions as determined by changing the 'div' parameter.
module top_module(
  input clk,
  input rst,
  output reg clk_out
);

code:
module top_module(
  input clk,
  input rst,
  output reg clk_out
);parameter div = 2;
reg [31:0] count;

always @(posedge clk or posedge rst) begin
  if (rst) begin
    clk_out <= 1'b0;
    count <= 0;
  end
  else begin
    if (count == div - 1) begin
      clk_out <= ~clk_out;
      count <= 0;
    end
    else begin
      count <= count + 1;
    end
  end
end

endmodule

